\title[The Analogy Distance]{The Analogy Distance:\\
An Attained Metric on Finite Probabilistic Transition Systems}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a quantitative theory of \emph{approximate structural analogy} between finite
probabilistic transition systems and show that it is governed by a genuine, attained
metric. An $\varepsilon$-approximate structural analogy between two systems is a
bijection of world sets preserving atomic truth probabilities exactly and matching the
transported one-step kernels to within total variation $\varepsilon$. We prove a
transport theorem: along such an analogy the truth probability of any modal formula of
depth $d$ moves by at most $1-(1-\varepsilon)^d$, a geometric modulus attained at every
depth by an explicit two-state leaking family; the natural linear guess $d\varepsilon$ is
a valid bound but is never attained for $d\ge 2$ and $0<\varepsilon<1$, while agreeing
with the true modulus to first order with discrepancy at most
$\tfrac{d(d-1)}{2}\varepsilon^{2}$. Approximate analogies form a groupoid graded by total
variation, and the induced least admissible defect $d(M,N)$ is an \emph{attained}
minimum over the finitely many atom-preserving renamings: an $\varepsilon$-approximate
analogy exists precisely when $d(M,N)\le\varepsilon$. The function $d$ takes values in
$[0,1]$, satisfies the metric axioms, and vanishes exactly on isomorphic pairs, so it is
a genuine metric on isomorphism classes. Consequently the map sending a system to its
vector of depth-$d$ truth probabilities is uniformly continuous with the optimal modulus
$1-(1-d(M,N))^{d}$, attained because the extremal family satisfies
$d(\text{exact},\text{leaky}(\varepsilon))=\varepsilon$. Finally we delimit the
observational cost of recovering structure: modal truth-probability equivalence is
strictly coarser than structural analogy, graded counting modalities do not close the
gap, and in nominal systems $\eta$-agreement on the depth-one fragment yields an
$(n\eta/2)$-approximate analogy with the dimension factor $n/2$ optimal.
\end{abstract}

